# Unification of Germany and Italy: Making Nations in the 19th Century

> In 1800 "Germany" and "Italy" were names on maps, not countries —
> a quilt of kingdoms, duchies and free cities. Within seventy years
> both had welded themselves into single states, one forged by "blood
> and iron", the other by diplomacy, rebellion and a red-shirted
> guerrilla. Together they redrew Europe's political geometry.

## 1. Europe Before Unification: Fragments, Not Nations

After Napoleon's defeat, the **Congress of Vienna (1815)** rebuilt
Europe on the principle of **legitimacy and balance** — and
consciously froze the fragmentation of Germany and Italy to prevent
any new hegemon.

- **Germany**: 39 sovereign states in a loose **German
  Confederation**, Austria and Prussia dominant; a customs union (the
  **Zollverein**, 1834) had already tied the economies together while
  politics stayed divided.
- **Italy**: before 1859 it was "a geographical expression" (Metternich):
  Austrian-ruled Lombardy–Venetia; a papal state; the Kingdom of the
  Two Sicilies; and northern duchies under Habsburg influence.

## 2. Germany: The Road Through Three Wars

[definition]
German unification was engineered by **Otto von Bismarck**, Prussian
Prime Minister from 1862, whose "Blood and Iron" speech declared that
Germany would be united not by speeches and votes but by war. The
result was a **Prussian-led, top-down unification — kleindeutsch**
(Germany without Austria) — proclaimed in January 1871.
[/definition]

| Step | Year | Event |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Army reform crisis | 1862–64 | Bismarck governs without parliament; builds the army |
| Danish War | 1864 | Prussia + Austria seize Schleswig-Holstein |
| Austro-Prussian War | 1866 | Seven Weeks' War; Austria excluded from German affairs |
| North German Confederation | 1867 | Prussia annexes/absorbs northern states |
| Franco-Prussian War | 1870–71 | southern states join against France |
| German Empire proclaimed | 1871 | at **Versailles**, Wilhelm I Kaiser; Bismarck Chancellor |

Why it succeeded: Prussian military power and rail-organised
mobilisation; the Zollverein's economic integration; nationalist
fever stoked against a common enemy (France); Bismarck's diplomatic
isolation of each victim in turn; and the **Ems Telegram** episode,
where Bismarck edited a dispatch to provoke France into declaring
war. The **Treaty of Frankfurt (1871)** took Alsace-Lorraine —
planting the revanche that fed the next war.

[important]
Contrast worth memorising: Germany unified **from above** — a
monarchy and army annexing states, conservative and calculated.
Italy unified **from below and outside** — romantic risings, a
popular hero, and a Piedmontese monarchy surfing on both. Same
result (nation-states by 1871), opposite mechanics.
[/important]

## 3. Italy: Cavour's Chess, Garibaldi's Sword

Four personalities anchor the story:

- **Mazzini** — the prophet: founded **Young Italy (1831)**; inspired
  republican nationalism; failed uprisings of 1831, 1848–49 (Roman
  Republic, defended by Garibaldi, crushed by the French).
- **Cavour** — the statesman: Prime Minister of **Piedmont-Sardinia**;
  modernised the economy; joined the **Crimean War (1855)** to buy a
  seat at the great-power table; made the **Pact of Plombières
  (1858)** with Napoleon III of France.
- **Garibaldi** — the soldier: with Mazzini and the martyred band of
  **Young Italy** behind him, he sailed with the **Thousand
  (Mille)** red-shirts in **1860**, conquering Sicily and Naples.
- **Victor Emmanuel II** — the king: Piedmont's throne became Italy's.

| Step | Year | Event |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Plombières pact | 1858 | France agrees to help against Austria |
| War with Austria | 1859 | Battles of Magenta, Solferino; Austria cedes Lombardy |
| Plebiscites | 1860 | Tuscany, Parma, Modena, Romagna join Piedmont |
| Expedition of the Thousand | 1860 | Garibaldi takes Sicily and Naples |
| Kingdom of Italy | 1861 | Victor Emmanuel II king; Turin capital |
| Venetia added | 1866 | via alliance with Prussia (Austro-Prussian War) |
| Rome added | 1870 | French garrison withdrawn (Franco-Prussian War); Rome capital 1871 |

The incomplete parts stayed incomplete: **Italia irredenta** —
Trentino and Trieste — remained Austrian until 1918, and the
**Roman Question** with the Pope (a self-declared "prisoner in the
Vatican") waited until Mussolini's Lateran Pacts of 1929.

## 4. The Human and Ideological Backdrop

Both unifications rode the century's great currents: **romantic
nationalism** (Herder's cultural nations; Mazzini's "duties of man"),
**liberal constitutionalism** (Frankfurt Parliament, 1848 — the
failed German attempt to unify by debate and crown-offered
constitutions), and **realpolitik** — Bismarck's cold art of the
possible that trumped ideology. The 1848 revolutions failed
everywhere in their grand aims, but they bankrupted the old order's
prestige and taught nationalists that armies and alliances, not
congresses, would finish the job.

## 5. Consequences

- **Balance of power shifted**: a united Germany (population,
  industry, the world's best army) displaced France as the
  continent's pivot; alliance systems followed (Bismarck's treaties,
  then the rival blocs) — the geometry that detonated in 1914.
- **France's revanche** over Alsace-Lorraine made Franco-German war
  a standing probability for two generations.
- **Italy's expectations vs reality**: unification delivered a state,
  not a nation — the north–south divide, banditry, and a franchise so
  narrow that few Italians had voted for their country. That gap
  between "made Italy" and "made Italians" (d'Azeglio's famous line)
  shadowed the state into the twentieth century.
- Both new states immediately joined the **scramble for colonies** —
  Germany in Africa and the Pacific, Italy embarrassingly at Adwa
  (1896) — feeding the imperial rivalries covered in the
  imperialism chapter.

## 6. Comparison Table

| | Germany | Italy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Architect | Bismarck (diplomacy + war) | Cavour (diplomacy) + Garibaldi (war) + Mazzini (idea) |
| Core state | Prussia | Piedmont-Sardinia |
| Wars | 1864, 1866, 1870–71 | 1859, 1860 (guerrilla), 1866, 1870 |
| Foreign help | none (isolated enemies) | France 1859; Prussia 1866/70 |
| Style | top-down, conservative | mixed; romantic risings + statecraft |
| Completed | 1871 (Versailles) | 1870–71 (Rome) |
| Left outside | Austria (kleindeutsch) | Trentino, Trieste (till 1918) |

## 7. Summary

Germany unified because Prussia wanted to be Germany — Bismarck
fought three calculated wars and proclaimed an empire at
Versailles. Italy unified because Piedmont surfed on Mazzini's idea
and Garibaldi's sword — Cavour traded alliances, the Thousand
conquered the south, and plebiscites stitched the boot together.
Both revealed the same truth: in the nineteenth century, nations were
made by railroads, rifles and realpolitik — and the Europe they made
was the Europe that went to war in 1914.
